A||N||5||MontanaFiero||Sequencing Turn Signals-- Cliff???||||5||
Z||000000||MontanaFiero||02-18-2001||05:20 AM||||Hey Cliff... or anyone else who has done this...<P>I just went to Radio Shack and bought the supplies to build the Fiero Sequencing Turn Signals circuit, including the pc board etching kit.  This is the one designed by Greg Piet. Cliff Pennock designed the printed circuit for it and it even prints out <I>backwords</I> so it could (theoretically) be transfered onto a pc board.<BR>Anyone have any suggestions on how to make this transfer? I used an HP deskjet to print on the paper. If I could get it to transfer onto the pc board, would the HP ink resist the etching solution?<P>Also, does this circuit <I>disable</I> the sequencing circuit for the <I>brake</I> lights like lawrence mazza's system does? (he takes the CLR pin on the timer and connects it to the turn signal indicator lamp).  <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/3/sts.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/3/sts.htm</A>  Any comments on this are appreciated.<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;<p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Greg Piet||02-18-2001||05:49 AM||||First things first...<BR>To use the PDF file you should print it onto an overhead transparency sheet to make the board.<P>There is a a reversed image so one can have the ink/toner that is attached to the overhead transparency be touching the metal part of the circuit board.  There are light activated circuit boards where the areas exposed to light get eaten up by the etching liquid.<P>This design and hookup has the brake lights sequencing as well.  With a few wires it should be possible to have a setup like Lawrence Mazza's setup, but since the brake light and turn signal light share the same filament it would require running another wire from under the dashboard of the car if I am not mistaken since that is the closest place where there is a differentiation between brakes and turn signals.<P>Uhh this has been discussed to the point of Forum members just  about telling me to shut the F%^K up so here are the links. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000604-2-000771.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000604-2-000771.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-001185.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-001185.html</A> <P>The second link talks about the PCB stuff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||MontanaFiero||02-18-2001||05:51 AM||||thanks Greg...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||MontanaFiero||02-18-2001||06:36 AM||||where do I get a pc board with "photo-resist"?||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||02-18-2001||09:26 AM||||The toner from a photocopier will work as an etch resist. Set the copier as dark as you can make it without distorting the patern. You can print the reverse image on standard paper and then iron it on to a standard copper-clad pc board. Run it under hot water to release the paper. rub it gently with your fingers to remove all the bits of paper from the board. Repair any broken traces with chartpak tape, radioshack rub-on transfers, or even a plain old Sharpie permanent marker. Now you're ready to etch. Ferric chloride stains pretty good, so be careful not to splash. I've done several pc boards using this method with great results. I have even seen boards layed out using nothing but a permanent marker. If I hadn't seen it work, I wouldn't have believed it. These methods should also work with photo etch board, just subject the board to UV light after the traces have been layed out.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000005||rockcrawl||02-18-2001||09:31 AM||||Edlie Electronics has photo etch and laser printer circuit board layout kits, but I don't see it on thier website <A HREF="http://www.edlieelectronics.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.edlieelectronics.com/</A>  I know it was in the catalog, but I can't find it now.||||reg||1
